{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you for tzitzit.</b> There is no clear meaning to this statement that it shall be for tzitzit. And according to the words of the Tosafot, who wrote in tractate Menaḥot (43a) in the name of Rabbi Meir who said there, and this is his language: the punishment for white is greater than the punishment for tekhelet — a parable to a king who said to two servants: to one he said, bring me a seal of clay, and to the other he said, bring me a seal of gold. Both transgressed and did not bring. Which of them has the greater punishment, etc. — the Tosafot wrote that what compares a seal of clay to tzitzit is that this is how they do for slaves, and the tzitzit testifies about Israel that they are servants of HaShem, as is stated in tractate Shabbat (57b): ״כַּבְלָא דְּעַבְדָא תְּנַן״ — a slave's cord, so far — the verse ״וְהָיָה לָכֶם לְצִיצִית״ intends, since they are servants of HaShem and the slave makes a cord, that this sign shall be for you a slave's cord." "And He said <b>and you shall see it</b> and you shall remember all the commandments of HaShem — meaning, when they look at the sign of their servitude, they will take to heart that they are not free men to do as they please in their eating, in their dress, in their speech, and in all their deeds — like a slave upon whom is the fear of his master, to stand when the master commands him to stand, and to perform all the labors that HaShem has commanded to do." "And He said <b>and you shall not stray</b> etc. — meaning, since that which is revealed is something the eye desires and craves, and the king's command is an injunction to do the opposite of the natural desire and to hate the felt sweetness — and it is impossible to withstand this except through the reminding of the sign of his servitude, which will force him to the contrary of what his eye and his heart desire there." "<b>And He said ״לְמַעַן תִּזְכְּרוּ וַעֲשִׂיתֶם״</b> — this may be explained according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Menaḥot 44a): an incident occurred with a certain man etc.; once he climbed up etc., his tzitzit struck him upon his face etc. And this is what ״לְמַעַן תִּזְכְּרוּ״ means — that the tzitzit will remind you and restrain you from sin, from every manner of sin. Therefore they were precise in saying <b>וַעֲשִׂיתֶם</b>, according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Qiddushin 39b): if a person sat and did not transgress, he is given reward like one who performs a commandment — and they established this in the case where a transgression came into his hand and he withdrew from it. And He said <b>וִהְיִיתֶם קְדוֹשִׁים</b> according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Vayikra Rabbah 24): one is not called holy unless he separates from forbidden relations. And His saying <b>אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם</b> etc. means — the binding obligation of servitude, that I obligate you to make upon it a sign, because I brought you out etc.; and He repeated saying a second time <b>אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם</b> — this is an intellectually grasped obligation. And see what I explained on the verse (Leviticus 25:55) ״כִּי לִי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל עֲבָדִים עֲבָדַי הֵם״ etc." "<b>And now</b> we come to grasp a matter of truth that HaShem commanded in this commandment: that one may not make tzitzit except in a garment with four corners, and not with three or with five. For since the tzitzit is a sign of servitude, HaShem commands that the sign also declare who our Master is — for this too is found among earthly kings, that each one makes a certain single sign for his servants and the sign of each is recognizable. Likewise HaShem decreed that we not make the sign of our servitude to HaShem except on a tallit with four corners, to allude that our King is the One who created the four corners of the world and rules over it. Therefore when the tallit has three or five corners, the allusion is lost and one may not make tzitzit on it." "<b>And He commanded</b> that the threads be white to mark out His signs, may He be blessed, for His attributes are the attribute of mercy and goodness, alluded to in the white species. Also the tekhelet to allude to His dominion in the heavens, for tekhelet resembles the firmament. Also in the number of the threads, four, which are eight, it alludes to His Name, may He be blessed, which is a Name of four, and its unification in His sanctuary is of eight, in the sod of the combination of הוי״ה and אדנות. Also He established in the law of El (Menahot 39a) to make a knot of tekhelet with the white, to awaken in a hidden concept, for the white is the sod of Ḥesed and the tekhelet is the sod of Rahamim, and it is the sod of the aspect of heaven. And this attribute is traced to our father Jacob, who is the sod of Tiferet, and those who understand the hidden things of the Torah said that the attribute of Tiferet cleaves to the attribute of Ḥesed, which is traced to Abraham; therefore the tekhelet surrounds the white, just as ru'aḥ surrounds the neshamah, as is known to those who know ḥen:" "<b>And it occurred to me</b> to note: why did HaShem not render fit the color of tekhelet except from a fish that comes up from the sea, and not from other things that have this very color? Perhaps it is for its own sake, for this is its sod, as they said, of blessed memory (Ḥullin 89a), the sea resembles the firmament — and understand. Also to note that mercies are drawn from the Torah, which is alluded to in the sea. Also to allude that mercies are drawn from the aspect alluded to in the fish, in the sod of the open eye, as is known to those who see in the Light of Life:" "<b>Thus is concluded the perashah of Shelah Leḥa</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ד׳ / ה׳ in עַבְדֵי ה׳ and similar has been rendered as HaShem per the voice rules for reverent abbreviations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:17.358916764Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}